Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick answer up front: SSNs don’t expire, so the SSN itself isn’t what determines a “too close” expiry for a six‑month visa. What usually matters is your passport expiry. Before I can check whether your dates are OK, I need to know which expiry date you mean — passport? visa? — and the actual expiry date.

Important safety note: please don’t post your full SSN in places that aren’t secure. You already included it here; going forward share only the last four digits if necessary.

Guidance you can use now
- SSN: it does not expire, and most forms only need the last 4 digits. If a form is asking for a full SSN, make sure the site is official and secure (HTTPS) and that it legitimately requires the full number.
- Passport expiry rule (most common rule): many countries require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond the date you intend to leave (or beyond your planned date of entry/stay). Some require three months or only that it covers the stay — check the specific country/consulate.
- Visa validity vs. passport validity: Even if the visa is valid for six months, the passport must usually meet the passport‑validity requirement above. A visa can’t be issued if your passport expires too soon.
- What to check: the embassy/consulate website for the destination country, the visa application instructions, and airline rules.

If you want me to check it precisely
